# Cursor Rules for ReTitle Extension

## Project Owner Context
**IMPORTANT:** The project owner is a product owner learning this technology stack. When providing explanations or making changes:
- Use clear, beginner-friendly language
- Explain "why" not just "what"
- Break down complex concepts
- Reference learning docs in `/docs/learning/` directory
- Be patient and thorough in explanations
- Suggest learning resources when introducing new concepts

## Project Overview
This is a Chrome Extension (Manifest V3) for renaming browser tab titles with persistence options. Built with TypeScript, Vite, React, Tailwind CSS, and shadcn/ui.

## Tech Stack
- **Runtime:** Chrome Extension (Manifest V3)
- **Language:** TypeScript (strict mode)
- **Build Tool:** Vite
- **UI Framework:** React 19
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- **Testing:** Playwright (E2E), Vitest (unit - recommended)
- **Package Manager:** npm

## Code Style & Standards

### TypeScript
- Use strict TypeScript (`strict: true` in tsconfig.json)
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use explicit return types for public functions
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Use type guards for runtime type checking
- Export types/interfaces from `src/shared/types.ts` when shared

### React (when using React components)
- Use functional components with hooks
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- Use TypeScript for component props: `interface ComponentProps { ... }`
- Use `React.memo` for expensive components
- Extract custom hooks to `src/hooks/` directory
- Use shadcn/ui components from `@/components/ui/`

### File Organization
```
src/
├── background/     # Service worker scripts
├── content/        # Content scripts
├── popup/          # Popup UI (migrating to React)
├── options/        # Options page (migrating to React)
├── components/     # Shared React components
│   └── ui/         # shadcn/ui components
├── lib/            # Utility functions (cn, etc.)
├── shared/         # Shared code (types, utils, messages)
└── styles/         # Global styles
```

### Naming Conventions
- **Files:** kebab-case for files (`storage-manager.ts`, `popup.tsx`)
- **Components:** PascalCase (`PopupApp`, `TitleInput`)
- **Functions:** camelCase (`saveTitle`, `getDomain`)
- **Constants:** UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (`MAX_STORAGE_SIZE`)
- **Types/Interfaces:** PascalCase (`StorageType`, `Message`)

## Chrome Extension Patterns

### Message Passing
- All messages must use typed interfaces from `src/shared/messages.ts`
- Use `createMessage()` helper for type-safe message creation
- Service worker message listeners MUST be at top level (MV3 requirement)
- Always return `true` from async message handlers

### Storage
- Use `StorageManager` class for all storage operations
- Storage types: `'once' | 'tab' | 'url' | 'domain'`
- Priority order: tab > url > domain > regex > bookmark
- Handle storage quota errors gracefully

### Service Worker
- Keep service worker stateless (MV3 requirement)
- Event listeners must be at top level
- Use in-memory cache for loop prevention (`titleCache`)
- Always handle errors in async operations

### Content Scripts
- Use `MutationObserver` to handle dynamic title changes
- Listen for `UPDATE_TITLE` messages from service worker
- Handle cases where content script isn't loaded yet

## Styling Guidelines

### Tailwind CSS
- Use Tailwind utility classes directly in components
- Prefer Tailwind classes over custom CSS
- Use `cn()` utility from `@/lib/utils` for conditional classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design

### shadcn/ui
- Add components via CLI: `npx shadcn@latest add [component]`
- Components live in `src/components/ui/`
- Customize components by editing files directly
- Use CSS variables for theming (defined in `globals.css`)

### Colors
- Primary: Use `primary` color from theme (blue)
- Use semantic colors: `destructive`, `muted`, `accent`
- Dark mode: Supported via CSS variables

## Error Handling

### Patterns
- Wrap async operations in try/catch
- Use `debugLog()` for development logging
- Return structured error responses: `{ success: false, error: string }`
- Show user-friendly error messages in UI
- Log technical details to console for debugging

### Error Messages
- User-facing: Clear, actionable messages
- Developer-facing: Include context and stack traces
- Use consistent error format across the codebase

## Testing Guidelines

### Page Object Model (POM)
- **Use POM pattern** for E2E tests (see `tests/POM_RECOMMENDATION.md`)
- Page objects live in `tests/pages/`
- One page object per page (PopupPage, OptionsPage, etc.)
- Use fixtures for extension loading (`tests/fixtures/extension.ts`)
- Use helpers for common operations (`tests/helpers/`)

### Page Object Structure
```typescript
// tests/pages/popup-page.ts
export class PopupPage extends BasePage {
  readonly titleInput = () => this.page.locator('#title-input');
  
  async enterTitle(title: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.titleInput().fill(title);
  }
}

// tests/e2e/popup.spec.ts
test('should save title', async ({ extensionContext, extensionId }) => {
  const popupPage = new PopupPage(await extensionContext.newPage());
  await popupPage.goto(extensionId);
  await popupPage.enterTitle('[PROD] {original}');
  await popupPage.save();
});
```

### Unit Tests
- Test utility functions in `src/shared/utils.ts`
- Test `StorageManager` methods
- Mock Chrome APIs using `@types/chrome` types
- Use Vitest for unit tests (recommended)

### E2E Tests
- Use Playwright for extension testing
- Use extension fixture for loading extension
- Test all storage types (once, tab, url, domain)
- Test priority system
- Test error scenarios
- Use page objects for all UI interactions

### Test Structure
```typescript
test.describe('FeatureName', () => {
  test.beforeEach(async ({ extensionContext }) => {
    // Setup
  });

  test('should do something', async ({ extensionContext, extensionId }) => {
    // Arrange
    const page = new PopupPage(await extensionContext.newPage());
    
    // Act
    await page.goto(extensionId);
    await page.enterTitle('Test');
    
    // Assert
    await expect(page.titleInput()).toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

### Extension Testing Best Practices
- Clear storage before each test
- Use `data-testid` attributes for stable selectors
- Wait for extension to load before testing
- Test in isolated contexts (popup, options, content)
- Use StorageHelper for setting up test data

## Performance Considerations

### Optimization Rules
- Debounce user input (title preview)
- Batch storage operations when possible
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Lazy load heavy dependencies
- Minimize bundle size (Chrome extension limits)

### Memory Management
- Clear caches when tabs are removed
- Clean up event listeners
- Avoid memory leaks in service worker

## Security Guidelines

### Input Validation
- Sanitize user input in title templates
- Validate regex patterns before saving
- Escape HTML in user-generated content
- Use Content Security Policy (CSP)

### Permissions
- Request minimum required permissions
- Document why each permission is needed
- Review permissions periodically

